Learning Movement and Basic Python Logic

Today I continued experimenting with K1, focusing on understanding how Python can be used to control its movements.
Using the ELECFREAKS booklet as a guide, I learned how to use the Pico's A and B buttons to control K1's motors.
I experimented with and, if and elif conditions, discovering that the order in which conditions are checked matters. 
Initially, pressing both buttons didn't produce the intended result because an earlier condition was being triggered first. After rearranging the conditions, I successfully programmed K1 to respond correctly.

I then experimented with automated movement using while True loops and sleep() commands.
By controlling each motor independently, I was able to make K1 turn on the spot. After experimenting with the timing, I successfully programmed K1 to drive around the floor in a repeating pattern.

What I Learned

How if and elif statements control program decisions.
How to check multiple conditions using and.
Why the order of conditions affects the result.
How, while True creates a repeating loop.
How sleep() controls the duration of movements.
How changing the direction of individual motors allows K1 to turn.

Reflection

Today was an important step towards understanding how to program K1 rather than simply copying instructions.
Seeing K1 perform a sequence of movements I'd programmed was incredibly satisfying. I'm beginning to understand how simple commands can be combined to produce more complicated behaviours.

